Advisory (not blocking; none cites a brief line, none reproduces a wrong merge):
A1. Hold-text clipping. hold() clips the whole reason at 400 chars, and why() already clips git's stderr at 200. With a long repo path (roughly over 125 chars) the trailing "partly updated: git -C <repo> status" recovery text, or the second occurrence of the path in the held text, is cut off. Realistic paths are far shorter. Suggest clipping only the dynamic part (stderr, path) and appending the fixed recovery tail after.
A2. False hold on a tracked file-to-directory swap. Reproduced: origin commit removes tracked a.txt and adds a.txt/b; local clean checkout gives ok:false "an incoming path would overwrite an untracked or ignored entry: a.txt/b; move it aside, then re-run". a.txt is tracked and the same merge deletes it, so git would handle it; the advice to "move it aside" is wrong for a tracked file and the hold cannot clear until the user acts. Fails closed (safe) and matches the literal step 9.2 wording ("ancestor exists as a non-directory"), but residual 2 says the guard "holds the untracked and ignored overlap only". Rare; worth a note or a `git ls-files --error-unmatch` exemption in a follow-on.
A3. Class note on the mechanism: every filesystem read in overwritten() is inside `repo` (the checkout being moved), which is the intended target, not the W-026 checkout-vs-branch-ref class; searched integrate.ts for git(repo,...) reads of refs: all use refs/heads/master, refs/remotes/origin/master or the literal oid, none reads the working copy for ref state.

